\begin{align*}
\sec^2 x (\cos^2 x) - \sec^2 x ((\cos^2 x)) &= \sec^2 x \cos^2 x - \sec^2 x \cos^2 x \\
&= 0.
\end{align*}
So the determinant is 0 when you subtract the second row from the first.

Since the determinant is 0, the matrix is singular and cannot be strictly invertible. This means that there is a non-trivial solution to the system of linear equations that corresponds to this matrix (which is given by solving the equations Set up these equations for the matrix here: \[
\begin{cases}
\sec^2 x y_1 + z_1 - z_2 &= 0 \\
\cos^2 x y_2 + \cos^2 x y_3 - \csc^2 x z_2 &= 0 \\
y_1 + \cos^2 x y_3 - \cot^2 x z_3 &= 0 \\
\end{cases}
\]
We can solve for one variable in terms of the other two, let's solve for $y_3$ in terms of $y_1$ and $z_1$:

