\boxed{ \sum_{k=1}^{1728} \frac{1}{1 + \omega^k + \omega^{2k} + \omega^{3k}} = \boxed{ \tfrac{1}{4} \left[ \boxed{\tfrac{1}{4} - 1 } \right] } }
\end{align*}
Note: I use $\omega$ to denote $e^{2\pi i/1729}$ throughout the calculation. I used $\omega = e^{2\pi i/1729}$ to generate a unit circle, i.e., $1,\omega, \omega^2,\dots, \omega^{1728},$ when I wrote the sum. Given our sum is $1728$ terms, I denote $\omega^{1729 + k} = \omega^k.$ This convention allows us to turn sums into closed forms since $1+\omega^{1729}+\omega^{1730}+\omega^{1731}\equiv 0.$

