&= \frac{1}{4} \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frac{1}{\cos^2(2\pi k / 1729)} \\
&= \frac{\pi}{2} \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \sec^2(2\pi k / 1729).
\end{align*}
We can use the identity $\sec^2 \theta = 1 + \tan^2 \theta$ to rewrite the sum as follows:
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \sec^2(2\pi k / 1729) &= \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} (1 + \tan^2(2\pi k / 1729)) \\
&= \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} 1 + \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \tan^2(2\pi k / 1729).
\end{align*}
The first sum is easily evaluated as $1728$. To evaluate the second sum, we first use the product-to-sum formula for cosines to write
\begin{align*}
\cos(2\pi (j-k) / 1729) &= \cos(2\pi j / 1729) \cos(2\pi k / 1729) + \sin(2\pi j / 1729) \sin(2\pi k / 1729) \\
\implies \cos^2(2\pi (j-k) / 1729) &= \frac{1}{2} (1 + \cos(4\pi (j - k) / 1729)).
\end{align*}
By summing over $j$, we get
