\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}
Let $\omega = e^{2 \pi i/1729}.$ We can write the sum as follows:
\[
\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frac{1}{1 + \omega^k + \omega^{2k} + \omega^{3k}} = \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \left(1 - \omega^{k} + \omega^{2k} - \omega^{3k}\right).
\]
Now we can rearrange the terms and group similar terms:
\[
\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \left(1 - \omega^{k} + \omega^{2k} - \omega^{3k}\right) = \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} 1 - \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \omega^{k} + \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \omega^{2k} - \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \omega^{3k}.
\]
The first sum, $\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} 1$, is equal to $1728$, and the other three sums are geometric series with the same common ratio $\omega$:

\vspace{1em}

The sum of the geometric series $\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \omega^{k}$: \hspace{1em}  $(1) \ \ \ \ \ $ \boxed{<1st Sum>}

\vspace{1em}

The sum of the geometric series $\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \omega^{2k}$: \hspace{1em}  $(2) \ \ \ \ \ $ \boxed{<2nd Sum>}

\vspace{1em}

The sum of the geometric series $\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \omega^{3k}$: \hspace{1em}  $(3) \ \ \ \ \ $  \boxed{<3rd Sum>}

\vspace{1em}

However, since $\omega^{1729} = 1$, we have that
\[
\omega^{1729 k} = 1 \cdot \omega^{k} \ (k = 1, 2, 3, \dots)
\]
 assuming any value of $k$. Using this fact, we can rewrite the first sum:
\begin{align*}
(1) &= \sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \omega^{k} = \sum_{k = 0}^{1728-1} \omega^{k} - \omega^{1728} \\
&= \sum_{k = 0}^{1727} \omega^{k} - 1.
\end{align*}

Similarly, we can rewrite the remaining two sums:
\begin{alignat*}{2}
(2) &= \sum_{k = 0}^{1727} \omega^{2k}, \ &&\text{ and, } \\
(3) &= \sum_{k = 0}^{1726} \omega^{3k}.
\end{alignat*}

At this point, we have found the expressions for each sum as a finite geometric series, and we can write the expression for the original sum as follows:

\begin{align*}
\sum_{k = 1}^{1728} \frac{1}{1 + \omega^k + \omega^{2k} + \omega^{3k}} &= 1728 - (1) - (2) - (3) \\
&= \boxed{1728 - \sum_{k = 0}^{1727} \omega^{k} - \sum_{k = 0}^{1727} \omega^{2k} - \sum_{k = 0}^{1726} \omega^{3k}}
\end{align*}
\end{document}
