 Use the following steps to solve the problem:

1. Compute the product of the first 20 positive integers.
   We will use the formula for the product of the first n positive integers: $n! = 1 \times 2 \times 3 \times ... \times n$.
   Using this, we have:
   \begin{align}
      \text{Product} &= \prod_{k=1}^{20} k \\
      &= 1 \times 2 \times 3 \times ... \times 20 \\
      &= \frac{20!}{(20-20)!} \\
      &= \frac{20!}{0!}
   \end{align}

2. Find all divisors of the product and eliminate those which are not relatively prime to it.
   We know that each integer between 1 and the square root of the product is a potential divisor. Since the product is even, we can further divide the potential divisors by 2. So the divisors we need to consider are odd integers in the range of $[1, \sqrt{20!}]$.
   We will use a loop to check for each potential divisor whether it divides the product or not.
   The loop will use the modulo operator (%) to check if the remainder when the product is divided by the divisor is 0.
   If the remainder is 0, then the divisor does divide the product, and we can mark it as not relatively prime.

3. Find the smallest positive integer that is greater than 1 and is relatively prime to the product.
   Since no divisor of the product was found to be relatively prime in the previous step, the smallest such integer must be the next integer after the last divisor checked. We can first get the last divisor found in the loop and then increment it by 1.

Here's the LaTeX code to implement the above steps:

```
% LaTeX code to find the smallest positive integer relatively prime to the product of the first 20 positive integers.

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{xinttools}

\begin{document}

\begin{itemize}

\item Compute the product of the first 20 positive integers:

\begin{align}
   \text{Product} &= \prod_{k=1}^{20} k \\
   &= 1 \times 2 \times 3 \times ... \times 20 \\
   &= \frac{20!}{(20-20)!} \\
   &= \frac{20!}{0!} \\
   &= \boxed{\frac{20!}{0!}} \text{ (This value is too large to write in the document, but it can be calculated using software or a programming language.) }
\end{align}

\item Find all divisors of the product and eliminate those which are not relatively prime to it:

\begin{verbatim}
   \setcounter{MaxMatrixCols}{20}
   \def\mathbf#1{{\bf #1}}

   \Prod = \frac{20!}{0!} % Product value

   \foreach \i in { {\xintTheRange{1}{sqrt(\Prod)}} }
   {
      \ smelled[ \i ] = (\Prod \mod \i)==0 % Store if \i divides \Prod
   }

   \foreach \i [count=\n] in \smelled
   {
      \ifnum\n<20
         \smelled[\n] = \i % Unset the \n-th divisor marked as false
      \fi
   }

   \foreach \i [count=\n] in \smelled
   {
      \ifnum\n=1
         \Divisors = \i
      \fi
   } % Get the last divisor found (which is not relatively prime to the product)

   % Now, find the next integer after the last divisor (i.e., the smallest positive integer relatively prime to the product):
   \NextDivisor = \Divisors + 1
\end{verbatim}

\item The smallest positive integer that is relatively prime to the product is \boxed{\NextDivisor}

\end{itemize}

\end{document}
```

