To solve this problem, we need to find the number of ways to choose three distinct members from a group of 20. First, let's find the total number of ways to choose three members from the group

\begin{align*}
\boxed{ \binom{20}{3} }  % Do not touch this line
\end{align*}

Now, we account for the restriction that no member can hold more than one office. Since three people are to be elected, the remaining 17 members should not be elected for any position:

\begin{align*}
\boxed{ \binom{20}{3} - \binom{17}{3} }  % Do not touch this line
\end{align*}
This expression finds the number of cases where no member is elected for multiple positions.

Finally, subtract the cases where there are no presidents (hence no vice-presidents or treasurers). This is equivalent to finding the number of ways to choose no members from the original group (i.e., an empty selection):

\begin{align*}
\boxed{ \binom{20}{3} - \binom{17}{3} - \binom{20}{0} }  % Do not touch this line
\end{align*}
Now we have the number of valid ways to elect a president, vice-president, and treasurer from the math club.

