In this problem, we are given that $|z| = 5$, i.e., $a^2 + b^2 = 25$. Recall that in the Cartesian plane, we represent the complex number $a + bi$ as a point $(a, b)$. Since $|z| = 5$, we know that the point representing $z$ lies on a circle centered at the origin with radius 5.

To find $|\overline{z}^2|$, we first compute $\overline{z}^2$, and then square it. Since we don't lose information when squaring, we can find the square ourselves:
\begin{align*}
\overline{z}^2 &= a^2 - 2abi - b^2 \\
&= 25 - 10ab \\
&= 25 - 10(x)(y) \text{ (where we let } z = x + yi)
\end{align*}

Now, $|\overline{z}^2|$ can be found by taking the square root of the squared magnitude of $\overline{z}^2$ (remember that the magnitude is the square root of the sum of the squares of its real and imaginary parts):
\begin{align*}
|\overline{z}^2| &= \sqrt{(25 - 10xy)^2 + (0)^2} \\
&= \sqrt{625 - 100xy^2 + 100x^2y^2 + 400x^2y^2}\\
&= \sqrt{(25 + 400x^2y^2) - 100xy^2 - 100x^2y^2}\\
\end{align*}

Since we are looking for the simplest exact value, recall that complex numbers themselves do not have units, so the units will be eliminated when we square the magnitude again:
