\begin{equation}
r^2 = \frac{\sin^2 x}{\cos^2 x} = \tan^2 x \quad (3)
\end{equation}

Substitute equation (3) into equation (2):
\begin{equation}
\tan x = \sqrt{r^2} \cdot \cos x \quad (4)
\end{equation}

Now, square both sides of equation (4):
\begin{equation}
\tan^2 x = \cos^2 x \cdot r^2 \quad (5)
\end{equation}

Finally, we will express $\cos^2 x$ in terms of $\tan^2 x$:
\begin{equation}
\cos^2 x = 1 - \tan^2 x \quad (6)
\end{equation}

Now that we have $\cos^2 x$ in terms of $\tan^2 x$, we can substitute it into the original problem:

\boxed{ \cos^3 x + \cos^2 x = \cos^3 x + (1 - \tan^2 x) }

