The solution to this problem is based on the periodicity of the sine function:
\begin{align*}
\sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) &= \sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (\sin (4-2 \pi)) + \sin^{-1} (\sin (5-2 \pi)) \\
&= \sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (-\sin 2) + \sin^{-1} (-\sin 1).
\end{align*}
Now we can find the solutions:
\begin{align*}
\sin^{-1} (\sin 3) &= \sin^{-1} \left(\sin \frac{3 \pi}{3}\right) = \frac{3 \pi}{3} = \frac{\pi}{1}. \\
\sin^{-1} (\sin 2) &= \sin^{-1} \left(\sin \frac{2 \pi}{2}\right) = \frac{2 \pi}{2} = \pi. \\
\sin^{-1} (\sin 1) &= \sin^{-1} \left(\sin \frac{1 \pi}{2}\right) = \frac{1 \pi}{2}.
\end{align*}
Therefore,
\begin{equation*}
\boxed{\sin^{-1} (\sin 3) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 4) + \sin^{-1} (\sin 5) = 0.}
\end{equation*}

