To project onto the $y$-axis, we need to discard the $x$-component, which means subtracting the $xy$ component of the vector from itself. We can create a $2x2$ matrix for this transformation:

\begin{equation*}
A =
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & -1 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation*}

Now we solve for the matrix that corresponds to this transformation:

\begin{equation*}
\begin{bmatrix}
y \\
0
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
0 & -1 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation*}

Rearranging, we get a system of linear equations:

\begin{align*}
y &= 0x - y \\
0 &= x + y
\end{align*}

From the second equation, we see that $y = -x$. We can substitute this into the first equation to find that $y = 0(-x) - y$, and thus $0 = y + y$, which means $y = 0$. Therefore, the matrix $A$ projects a vector onto the $y$-axis by discarding the x-component and keeping the y-component; in other words, it simply copies the y-component and sets the x-component to zero.

To represent this transformation using a matrix, we can change the basis so that the new basis vectors are ($0, 1$) and ($1, 0$). These bases correspond to projecting onto the $y$-axis and the $x$-axis, respectively. Given the change of basis matrix between the old and new bases, we can find the matrix for projection onto the $y$-axis:

Let $Q$ be the change of basis matrix. Then:

\begin{align*}
Q \begin{bmatrix}
1 \\
0
\end{bmatrix} &= \begin{bmatrix}
0 \\
1
\end{bmatrix} \\
Q \begin{bmatrix}
0 \\
1
\end{bmatrix} &= \begin{bmatrix}
1 \\
0
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}

This gives us the following system of linear equations:

\begin{align*}
\begin{bmatrix}
q_{11} \\
q_{21}
\end{bmatrix} &= \begin{bmatrix}
0 \\
1
\end{bmatrix} \\
\begin{bmatrix}
q_{12} \\
q_{22}
\end{bmatrix} &= \begin{bmatrix}
1 \\
0
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}

Solving this system gives us $Q = \begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}$. However, this means that the basis we chose for our projection is simply a rotation of the standard basis, and an easy way to project onto the $y$-axis would be to discard the x-component of the vector as we did earlier.

To find the projector matrix $P_y$, we can use the matrix of the identity operator subtracted from the matrix of the basis change $Q$.

\begin{equation*}
Q = \begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix} \quad , \quad I = \begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
P_y = Q - I = \begin{bmatrix}
0 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation*}

With this projector matrix, we can now project any vector onto the $y$-axis by multiplying it by this matrix:

\begin{equation*}
\boxed{P_y \begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
0 \\
y
\end{bmatrix}}
\end{equation*}

