We'll start with the given vector in three dimensions, represented as a column vector:

\begin{equation*}
\textbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation*}

The vector we're looking for is the projection of $\textbf{v}$ onto the $y$-axis, and the $y$-axis is represented by a vector perpendicular to the $xy$-plane, which is called the z-hat vector:

\begin{equation*}
\hat{\textbf{k}} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation*}

Recall the formula for projection:

\begin{equation*}
\textbf{proj}_{\hat{\textbf{k}}} \textbf{v} = \frac{\textbf{v} \cdot \hat{\textbf{k}}}{\hat{\textbf{k}}^T \hat{\textbf{k}}} \hat{\textbf{k}}
\end{equation*}

Suppose a point $\textbf{v}=<x,y,z>$. Now, let's calculate its projection:

\begin{align*}
\textbf{proj}_{\hat{\textbf{k}}} \textbf{v} &= \frac{\textbf{v} \cdot \hat{\textbf{k}}}{\hat{\textbf{k}}^T \hat{\textbf{k}}} \hat{\textbf{k}} \\
&= \left( \frac{0\text{ }y\text{ }z}{\sum_{i=1}^3 (0)^2} \right) \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \frac{y}{1} \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \\
&= \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ y \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}

Since the projection is simply a scalar multiple of $\hat{\textbf{k}}$, we can rewrite the projection matrix $\textbf{P}$ as:

\begin{equation*}
\textbf{P} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation*}

Thus, to project a vector $\textbf{v}$ onto the $y$-axis, multiply it by the \boxed{<matrixP>} matrix. Note that the matrix has dimensions of 3x3, but the unit is not relevant here since we're dealing with 3D Euclidean space.

