Solution:
The matrix representing a projection onto a given axis is given by the outer product of the normalized vector of that axis. For the $y$-axis, the normalized vector is $\hat{y} = (0, 1)$. Therefore, the matrix is:

\boxed{ \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} }

This is a projection matrix because it satisfies the property of projecting a vector onto the $y$-axis:

A projection matrix P has the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item PP = P ( idempotency)
\item P*A = A*P  (commutativity with multiplication)
\end{enumerate}

Let us verify those properties for our solution:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $ \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} * \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}$, so it is indeed idempotent.
\item For any matrix A = $\begin{bmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \\ e & f \\ g & h \\ \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} * A = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & d \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{bmatrix} * \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}$. So it commutes with multiplication.
\end{enumerate}

Done.

