By comparing the expressions for x and y, we can see that if m and n are chosen so that $\frac{21}{6} - m$ and $\frac{21}{7} - n$ leave small remainders when divided by $2\pi$, then x and y will be close to each other, and setting them equal will solve the equation.

Now we can test some numbers for m and n until we find the values for which the expressions on the left and right are equal (or close enough):

When m = 3 and n = 4:

\boxed{ \frac{21\theta}{6} - 6\pi = \frac{21\theta}{7} - 4\pi }

Multiplying both sides by 21, and combining the terms:

\boxed{ 21\theta - 126\pi = 21\theta - 84\pi }

