  Here is a step-by-step solution: Let's denote the side length of the original cube as x. The volume of the original cube can be calculated as follows:

\boxed{ V_1 = x^3 = 2^3 = 8 \text{ cubic centimeters } }

The volume of the new cube is 8 + 19 = 27 cubic centimeters. Let's denote the side length of the new cube as y. The volume of the new cube can be calculated as follows:

\boxed{ V_2 = y^3 = 27 }

Since the volume has increased by 19 cubic centimeters, we have an equation for y:

\boxed{ V_2 - V_1 = y^3 - 8 = 19 }

Now we need to solve for y:
\begin{align*}
y^3 - 8 &= 19 \\
y^3 &= 27 \Rightarrow y = 3
\end{align*}

The length of one edge of the new cube is \boxed{ 3 \text{ centimeters } }.
